\details{
\subsection{Why this function exists}{

The most reliable way to isolate emergency department visits from
non-emergency providers is to filter to a valid \code{FacilityType} before or
during case counting. Where a site's facilities are consistently onboarded
to ESSENCE, restricting the query itself to \code{FacilityType = "Emergency Care"} (a front-end filter, applied before the pull) avoids returning
non-ED provider data at all. This approach worked reliably for years in
Kentucky -- until several free-standing emergency departments (FSEDs) were
onboarded to ESSENCE with a \code{FacilityType} other than \code{"Emergency Care"}.
A front-end query filtered to \code{FacilityType = "Emergency Care"} now
silently excludes these FSEDs' legitimate ED visits, even though each
operates a true emergency department.

Once front-end filtering by \code{FacilityType} can no longer be trusted for a
site, a raw pull returns every facility type that submitted matching
records -- including primary care clinics, specialty practices, and other
genuinely non-ED providers whose visits should not contribute to an
ED-based numerator or denominator. \code{filter_care_setting()} addresses this
by first correcting the small, known set of misclassified facilities (by
name, ID, or pattern) to a valid ED-consistent \code{FacilityType}, then
filtering to \code{keep_types}. This makes it possible to filter reproducibly
and defensibly on \code{FacilityType} again, without excluding true ED visits
or manually rebuilding the correction list for every pull. Whether this
pre-cleaning step is worthwhile depends on a site's own onboarding
consistency -- sites where \code{FacilityType} reliably identifies emergency
providers may not need it at all.
}

\subsection{FSED facility type assignment}{

The specific pattern observed in Kentucky's ESSENCE data is that some
FSEDs are onboarded with a \code{FacilityType} of \code{"Urgent Care"} rather than
\code{"Emergency Care"}. This reflects what has been observed and processed in
Kentucky's data specifically -- it is not a documented or guaranteed
convention across all NSSP sites, and other sites may see FSEDs (or other
facility types) onboarded under different \code{FacilityType} values entirely.
Regardless of which value a given site observes, the
\code{fix_facility_type_vector}, \code{fix_facility_id_vector}, and
\code{fix_facility_type_regex} parameters exist to reassign known misclassified
facilities to a specified \code{fix_to} value before the \code{keep_types} filter is
applied.
}

\subsection{Kentucky data structure}{

In Kentucky, inpatient admission data are transmitted through the
corresponding ED hospital feeds and share the same \code{FacilityType}. The
default \code{keep_types} of \code{c("Emergency Care", "Inpatient Practice Setting")}
reflects this structure. Sites pulling ED visits only should set
\code{keep_types = "Emergency Care"}.
}

\subsection{Processing order}{

Corrections are applied before filtering, in this sequence:
\enumerate{
\item Exact ID corrections (\code{fix_facility_id_vector})
\item Exact name corrections (\code{fix_facility_type_vector})
\item Regex name corrections (\code{fix_facility_type_regex})
\item Filter to \code{keep_types}
}

ID and name corrections are both exact-match methods and take precedence
over regex -- a facility corrected by either \code{fix_facility_id_vector} or
\code{fix_facility_type_vector} will not be re-evaluated by
\code{fix_facility_type_regex}, even if its name also happens to match the
pattern.
}

\subsection{Facility ID vs. facility name corrections}{

\code{fix_facility_id_vector} matches on \code{facility_id_col} (\code{Hospital} /
\code{C_BioSense_Facility_ID} by default), which does not change even when a
facility's name is edited or the facility is rebranded. For a correction
list reused across many recurring pulls, ID-based correction is more
durable than name-based correction. \code{fix_facility_type_vector} remains
available for cases where the ID isn't known or \code{Hospital} wasn't
included as a pull field. Both accept the same \code{fix_to} value and can be
used together.
}

\subsection{dry_run preview}{

When \code{dry_run = TRUE}, the function returns a preview tibble showing each
facility's original and corrected \code{FacilityType}, visit count, and whether
it would be retained -- without modifying the data. Use this to verify
corrections and \code{keep_types} before committing to a filter.
}

\subsection{Warnings and dry_run}{

Facilities matched and corrected via \code{fix_facility_type_regex} are always
surfaced in a warning, since regex matching is open-ended -- a newly
onboarded facility could start matching the pattern at any time, which is
worth knowing about on every run, not just during setup. These are
candidates for promotion to \code{fix_facility_type_vector} or
\code{fix_facility_id_vector} for explicitness and long-term reproducibility.

Unmatched entries in \code{fix_facility_type_vector} (a name with zero matching
rows in this pull) only produce a warning when \code{dry_run = TRUE}. A
persistent correction list reused across many pulls will routinely include
facilities with zero visits in a given pull -- this is expected, not an
error, so it isn't surfaced on ordinary (non-\code{dry_run}) runs. \code{dry_run}
is the intended point to verify a correction list is behaving as expected.
Unmatched entries in \code{fix_facility_id_vector} never produce a warning, at
any setting, for the same reason.
}
